    {
      "id": "p0166-b003",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "An elderly turtle named Gerson runs a shop in Waterfall. In English, he speaks like a kooky old man. This attribute was retained in Japanese by giving him an equivalent \"stereotypical old person\" speaking style. Gerson also uses the Japanese pronoun washi, which is commonly used by old characters in Japanese entertainment.",
        "raw": "An elderly turtle named Gerson runs a shop in Waterfall. In English, he speaks\nlike a kooky old man. This attribute was retained in Japanese by giving him\nan equivalent \"stereotypical old person\" speaking style. Gerson also uses\nthe Japanese pronoun washi, which is commonly used by old characters in\nJapanese entertainment.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.29376236,
          0.27083571,
          0.895577,
          0.35635714
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.2917282,
          0.27012143,
          0.89761115,
          0.35742857
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          30,
          31,
          32,
          33,
          34
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "年迈的乌龟 Gerson 在瀑布开店。英语里，他像个古怪老头一样说话。日语通过相应的“典型老人”口吻保留了这个特点。Gerson 还使用日语第一人称 washi（我），这也是日本娱乐作品中老年角色常用的自称。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": [
          {
            "start": 75,
            "end": 80,
            "italic": true,
            "lang": "en"
          }
        ]
      },
